### 9. Monthly Breakdown of Anomalous Year 2019
* **Code:**
  ```powershell
  $imergData = (Get-Content nasapower.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json).properties.parameter.IMERG_PRECTOT
  $weatherData = (Get-Content nasapower_weather.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json).properties.parameter.PRECTOTCORR

  $months = 1..12
  $mResults = @()
  foreach ($m in $months) {
      $mStr = "{0:D2}" -f $m
      $prefix = "2019$mStr"
      $iSum = 0
      $mSum = 0
      foreach ($prop in $imergData.PSObject.Properties) {
          if ($prop.Name.StartsWith($prefix)) { $iSum += [double]$prop.Value }
      }
      foreach ($prop in $weatherData.PSObject.Properties) {
          if ($prop.Name.StartsWith($prefix)) { $mSum += [double]$prop.Value }
      }
      $mResults += [PSCustomObject]@{
          Month = $mStr
          IMERG_mm = [math]::Round($iSum, 1)
          MERRA_mm = [math]::Round($mSum, 1)
      }
  }
  $mResults | Format-Table -AutoSize
  ```
* **Reason:** Test whether the apparent delayed onset in 2019 observed in MERRA-2 was supported by IMERG.
* **Result:** MERRA-2 showed dry April–June (40–55 mm/mo), whereas IMERG revealed substantial early rain: March (205.5 mm), April (104.3 mm), May (157.8 mm), and June (126.1 mm).


